From da91716e59d54a741802bb37954a4b9df0e46e00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:02:08 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: the input CBUF surface stride was an integer
 division

surf was inw * ic / 64. A row costs inw * DIV_ROUND_UP(ic, 16) feature atomics
of 16 bytes and this counts them in 64 byte units, so it is inw * atomics / 4.
The two agree only when ic is a multiple of 16, and the old form truncates.

At 32 channels that costs half a unit on every odd width, and on the board
every odd width failed while every even one passed, with nothing else to
separate them: dw32w71 was 2 of 32 channels and dw32w72 beside it 32 of 32,
one row window each and well inside the CBUF budget.

The derived form reproduces the old value exactly on all ten shapes that
already passed, at 16, 32, 64, 128 and 1024 channels, and changes only the
three that failed.

Board: dw32w71, dw32w109 and dw32w111 go from 2, 0 and 0 of 32 channels to 32
of 32, dw32w31 passes, and the even widths beside them do not move. The
sharpest check is dw64w71, an odd width where surf was already exact because
64 channels divide evenly: it passes on both settings, which it must if the
truncation is the mechanism. ROCKET_SURF_TRUNC restores the old form and
breaks dw32w71 again.

Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
index 60a05df..806513a 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_regcmd.c
@@ -556,7 +556,29 @@ fill_regcmd_rk3576_normal(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
    bool capped = win_orows < full_oh;           /* a window of a larger output */
    unsigned wnum = task->num;
 
-   unsigned surf = inw * ic / 64;                /* input CBUF surface stride */
+   /*
+    * Input CBUF surface stride. A row costs inw * DIV_ROUND_UP(ic, 16) feature
+    * atomics of 16 bytes, and this counts them in 64 byte units, so it is
+    * inw * atomics / 4. Writing it as inw * ic / 64 is the same thing only
+    * when ic is a multiple of 16, and it truncates.
+    *
+    * Board: at 32 channels the truncation costs half a unit on every ODD
+    * width, and every odd width measured fails while every even one passes.
+    * dw32w71 is 2 of 32 channels and dw32w72 beside it is 32 of 32, one row
+    * window each and well inside the CBUF budget, so nothing else separates
+    * them. 64 channels never truncates, inw * 4 / 4 being exact, and 64
+    * channel layers fail for a different reason.
+    *
+    * This form reproduces the old value exactly on every case that passes
+    * today: 112 wide at 16, 32 and 64 channels, 96 wide at 16, 32 and 64, 44
+    * wide at 128, and 7 wide at 1024.
+    *
+    * ROCKET_SURF_TRUNC=1 restores the truncating form.
+    */
+   unsigned surf = getenv("ROCKET_SURF_TRUNC")
+                      ? inw * ic / 64
+                      : DIV_ROUND_UP(inw * DIV_ROUND_UP(ic, FEATURE_ATOMIC_SIZE),
+                                     4);
    unsigned wbpk = dw ? (k * k * ic / 8) : (ic * k * k * 2); /* weight bytes/kernel */
    /* k_word tracks the kernel extent: per-byte = (k-1) (k=3 -> 0x0202, k=5 ->
     * 0x0404). Was hard-coded to the k=3 value, which mis-fetched weights for k=5. */
